Turn the first-run page into a setup screen: presets, import, and a flag fix.

The page already told people what Aerium had decided for them and listed flags
they could go and set by hand. This makes it do the setting - three presets, one
Apply button - and adds an entry point for bringing a previous browser's data
across.

Presets
-------
Recommended (pre-selected), Privacy, and Performance. All three share a floor:
Do Not Track, third-party cookies blocked, hyperlink auditing pings never sent,
failed navigations never handed to an error-resolution web service, sites unable
to probe whether a payment method is saved, HTTPS-First in its balanced
configuration, WebRTC confined to the public interface, no search-engine
collection from visited sites, Client Hints stripped, Memory Saver on, and no
background process left behind after the last window closes. They differ in what
they trade away:

  - Recommended keeps page preloading off and search suggestions on, and leaves
    Battery Saver on battery power. It is deliberately the boring one: nothing
    in it should ever make a site misbehave.

  - Privacy adds delete-on-exit, turns search suggestions off so nothing typed
    in the address bar leaves the browser at all, moves HTTPS-First from
    balanced to strict, refuses non-proxied UDP for WebRTC, and switches on the
    referrer-stripping, fingerprinting-noise, WebGL-spoofing,
    reduced-system-info, punycode and incognito-quota flags. Every one of those
    is a real tradeoff, which is why it is a separate choice rather than the
    default.

  - Performance turns page preloading on (kStandard), enables parallel
    downloading, raises the per-host connection ceiling from 6 to 15, makes
    Memory Saver aggressive so idle tabs give their memory back sooner, and
    switches Battery Saver off so nothing is throttled when unplugged. The
    privacy floor is unchanged - none of it costs measurable speed.

Why strict HTTPS-First is Privacy-only
--------------------------------------
aerium-https-first-balanced-auto-enable.patch already makes the case: strict
mode (kHttpsOnlyModeEnabled) also warns on private and local hosts - router
admin pages, localhost, NAS boxes, printers, intranet names - which cannot hold
a publicly trusted certificate and so become an interstitial on every visit.
That is the wrong default, and precisely the sort of deliberate tradeoff the
Privacy preset exists to hold.

All three presets write kHttpsFirstBalancedMode explicitly, which is also what
makes balanced mode actually take effect: IsBalancedModeEnabled() in
chrome/browser/ssl/https_upgrades_util.cc only consults the pref once
HasPrefPath() is true, and before that falls through to
kHttpsFirstBalancedModeAutoEnable. Writing the pref moves the decision from a
feature flag to the user's own setting, which is where it belongs.

WebRTC IP handling
------------------
ungoogled-chromium ships disable_non_proxied_udp, which is the strongest
setting and also the one that breaks voice and video calls whenever no TURN
relay is reachable. Recommended and Performance write
default_public_interface_only instead: local interface addresses - the actual
deanonymisation vector, and the thing the flag exists for - are still never
enumerated, and calls still connect. Privacy keeps disable_non_proxied_udp,
because a proxy or VPN that UDP walks straight past is not a proxy.

Prefs
-----
Every pref is written from C++ against the real PrefService, so the
settingsPrivate allowlist in prefs_util.cc is not involved (that gate only
applies to chrome://settings, which reaches prefs through the extension API).
Each was checked to exist and to be registered at 151.0.7922.108:

  enable_do_not_track                            bool, profile
      chrome/common/pref_names.h, registered by RegisterBrowserUserPrefs in
      chrome/browser/ui/browser_ui_prefs.cc
  profile.cookie_controls_mode                   int, profile
      components/content_settings/core/common/pref_names.h, registered by
      content_settings::CookieSettings::RegisterProfilePrefs.
      CookieControlsMode: kOff=0, kBlockThirdParty=1, kIncognitoOnly=2
  net.network_prediction_options                 int, profile
      registered by prefetch::RegisterPredictionOptionsProfilePrefs.
      NetworkPredictionOptions: kStandard=0, kWifiOnlyDeprecated=1,
      kDisabled=2, kExtended=3
  browser.clear_data.aerium_clear_on_exit        bool, profile
      added by aerium-clear-browsing-data-on-exit.patch, registered through
      browsing_data::prefs::RegisterBrowserUserPrefs
  search.suggest_enabled                         bool, profile
      chrome/common/pref_names.h, registered by Profile::RegisterProfilePrefs
  https_only_mode_enabled                        bool, profile
      chrome/common/pref_names.h (kHttpsOnlyModeEnabled), registered by
      RegisterBrowserUserPrefs
  https_first_balanced_mode_enabled              bool, profile
      chrome/common/pref_names.h (kHttpsFirstBalancedMode), registered by
      RegisterBrowserUserPrefs; aerium-https-first-balanced.patch changes its
      registered default to true
  webrtc.ip_handling_policy                      string, profile
      chrome/common/pref_names.h (kWebRTCIPHandlingPolicy), registered by
      RegisterBrowserUserPrefs. The four legal values are the
      blink::kWebRTCIPHandling* constants in
      third_party/blink/common/peerconnection/webrtc_ip_handling_policy.cc:
      "default", "default_public_and_private_interfaces",
      "default_public_interface_only", "disable_non_proxied_udp"
  enable_a_ping                                  bool, profile
      chrome/common/pref_names.h (kEnableHyperlinkAuditing), registered by
      ChromeContentBrowserClient::RegisterProfilePrefs
  alternate_error_pages.enabled                  bool, profile
      components/embedder_support/pref_names.h, registered by
      ProfileNetworkContextService::RegisterProfilePrefs
  payments.can_make_payment_enabled              bool, profile
      components/payments/core/payment_prefs.h, registered by
      payments::RegisterProfilePrefs from RegisterProfilePrefs in
      chrome/browser/prefs/browser_prefs.cc
  performance_tuning.high_efficiency_mode.state           int, LOCAL STATE
      MemorySaverModeState: kDisabled=0, kDeprecated=1, kEnabled=2
  performance_tuning.high_efficiency_mode.aggressiveness  int, LOCAL STATE
      MemorySaverModeAggressiveness: kConservative=0, kMedium=1, kAggressive=2
  performance_tuning.battery_saver_mode.state             int, LOCAL STATE
      BatterySaverModeState: kDisabled=0, kEnabledBelowThreshold=1,
      kEnabledOnBattery=2, kEnabled=3
      All three are declared in
      components/performance_manager/public/user_tuning/prefs.h and registered
      by its RegisterLocalStatePrefs - not by its RegisterProfilePrefs, which
      only registers the tab-discard exception lists.
  background_mode.enabled                                 bool, LOCAL STATE
      chrome/common/pref_names.h (kBackgroundModeEnabled), registered by
      BackgroundModeManager::RegisterPrefs under
      BUILDFLAG(ENABLE_BACKGROUND_MODE), which chrome/common/features.gni
      defines as !is_android && !is_chromeos - true for both desktop targets.

The performance prefs and background_mode.enabled are browser-wide, not per
profile - they come from RegisterLocalStatePrefs, not RegisterProfilePrefs - so
they are written to g_browser_process->local_state() like the existing Secure
DNS handler does. Getting that distinction wrong is a CHECK failure inside
PrefService, not a silent no-op.

No fingerprinting-protection pref is set. There is no stock Chromium pref for
it at this revision; what Aerium can actually offer is the Bromite-derived
canvas/client-rects noise flags and ungoogled's WebGL spoofing, and those are
what the Privacy preset turns on. Nothing is promised in the UI that the
browser cannot deliver.

Flags
-----
Preset flags are appended to the local-state list about_flags reads at startup
(browser.enabled_labs_experiments, the same list default-flags.patch seeds
"extension-mime-request-handling@2" into). Existing entries are preserved and
duplicates skipped, so applying a preset never clobbers a hand-set flag. Entry
syntax follows FeatureEntry::NameForOption(): the option index is appended
after an "@", and which index means what depends on the entry type -
SINGLE_VALUE_TYPE has no options and is stored under its bare internal name;
ENABLE_DISABLE_VALUE_TYPE and FEATURE_VALUE_TYPE have three options, so "@1" is
Enabled; MULTI_VALUE_TYPE has one option per Choice; and
FEATURE_WITH_PARAMS_VALUE_TYPE has 3 + variations options, where "@1" is
Enabled with the feature's own default params. Each flag was confirmed to exist
at this revision:

  disable-search-engine-collection      SINGLE_VALUE          ungoogled
  force-punycode-hostnames              SINGLE_VALUE          ungoogled
  fingerprinting-canvas-image-data-noise     SINGLE_VALUE     bromite
  fingerprinting-canvas-measuretext-noise    SINGLE_VALUE     bromite
  fingerprinting-client-rects-noise          SINGLE_VALUE     bromite
  remove-client-hints                   FEATURE_VALUE         ungoogled
  reduced-system-info                   FEATURE_VALUE         ungoogled
  minimal-referrers                     FEATURE_VALUE         ungoogled
  increase-incognito-storage-quota      FEATURE_VALUE         ungoogled
  spoof-webgl-info                      FEATURE_WITH_PARAMS   ungoogled
  enable-parallel-downloading           FEATURE_VALUE         upstream about_flags.cc
  max-connections-per-host              MULTI_VALUE           bromite

Two of those need their index spelled out:

  spoof-webgl-info@1 is Enabled with no variation, so blink's
  kSpoofWebGLRendererParam and kSpoofWebGLVendorParam keep the defaults they are
  declared with in third_party/blink/common/features.cc - both " " - which is
  the same blank renderer and vendor the "Blank" variation would set. Picking
  the plain Enabled option rather than a named GPU is deliberate: claiming to be
  a specific card is a fingerprint of its own if few other users claim it.

  max-connections-per-host@1 is the second and last entry of
  kMaxConnectionsPerHostChoices in chrome/browser/bromite_flag_choices.h, which
  passes --max-connections-per-host=15. Index 0 is the Default choice.

Rejected while auditing the list: chrome://flags/#disable-top-sites exists as a
flag entry, but nothing at this revision reads the --disable-top-sites switch it
sets, so it would be a promise the browser does not keep. #no-pings is real, but
the enable_a_ping pref covers the same ground and takes effect without a
restart, so the pref is used instead.

While auditing that list: chrome://flags/#remove-tabsearch-button, which the
page has been recommending, does not exist. It is in neither about_flags.cc nor
any ungoogled-chromium/bromite flag header at this revision, so the bullet is
removed rather than left pointing at nothing.

Flags only take effect after a restart, unlike the prefs, and the status line
says so.

Import
------
No new import code. The link opens chrome://settings/importData, which is the
route people_page.ts already watches to show <settings-import-data-dialog> -
the same dialog the Settings "Import bookmarks and settings" row opens. That
row is exactly why aerium-remove-google-services-section.patch kept
importDataDialogTrigger while deleting everything around it.
